The master’s dharma name was Zhuhong; his courtesy name Fohui, and his sobriquet Lianchi, Lotus Pond — the place toward which his aspiration turned. His secular surname was Shen; he was a man of Renhe in ancient Hangzhou, and his family had been a distinguished one for generations. His father was Shen Dejian, styled Mr. Mingzhai; his mother was of the Zhou family. From birth the master was bright and uncommon, and the flavors of the world were bland to him. At seventeen he entered the county school, and in examinations he repeatedly came first among the scholars; by his learning and conduct he was held in weight by his generation. The degrees were his for the taking, yet his will was set on leaving the world, and he often wrote the four words “the matter of birth and death is great” on the desk before him. Whenever he joined companions in discoursing on letters, he would always bend the talk back to Buddhist principle; already his heart rested in the Pure Land. His household abstained from killing, and their offerings were always vegetarian. Often he would sigh: “Human life passes like a white colt glimpsed through a crack. How much is there in this floating life? If by thirty I have not succeeded, I shall surely go beyond, and go for good. Why spend a lifetime on what is petty and squalid?” His first wife, of the Zhang family, bore a son who died young; then his wife died, and he wished not to marry again, but his mother forced him. A match was proposed with a woman of the Tang family, a poor girl who ate only vegetables. A wealthy man who wanted the master as his own fine son-in-law secretly worked to part them, yet the master took Tang in the end; but in his heart he did not wish to complete the rites of husband and wife. At twenty-seven his father died; at thirty-one his mother died. Then he wept and said, “My parents’ kindness is boundless; this is the very time to repay it.” By now his resolve to depart for good was fixed. In the yichou year of Jiajing, on New Year’s Eve, the master asked Tang to make tea. She carried it to the desk, and the cup cracked. The master laughed and said, “There is no union of causes and conditions that does not scatter.” The next year, bingyin, he took leave of Tang: “Love and affection are not lasting; birth and death none may bear for another. I am going. Plan for yourself.” Tang answered with spirit, “You go first; I shall follow at my leisure.” The master then composed the lyric A Stroke That Crosses It All Out, and straightway went to the monk Xingtian Li and had his head shaved. He sought the vinaya master Wuchen Yu of Zhaoqing Monastery and received full ordination at the platform. After a short while he set out with a single bowl and one staff to wander the regions, visiting good and wise teachers everywhere. Journeying north to Wutai, he was moved by Mañjuśrī’s emission of light. At Funiu he joined the assembly in their austerities. Entering the capital, he called on the two great elders Bianrong and Xiaoyan, and from each he received an opening. Passing through Dongchang, words suddenly came to him, and he made a verse: “Twenty years ago the matter was in doubt;
three thousand li away, what a strange encounter;
burning incense and casting down the halberd were all a dream —
demon and Buddha strive in vain over right and wrong.” Because his mourning for his mother was not yet ended, he carried her wooden tablet with him as he wandered; at every meal he made an offering, and wherever he stayed he served it. Such was his grief and longing. At Waguan Monastery in Jinling he fell so ill that he nearly died; his companions were about to carry him to cremation when the master faintly said, “I still have one breath left.” So they stopped. When the illness eased he returned. In Yue there were many Chan retreats; the master joined five of them, and to the end never knew the name of the monk on the neighboring seat. In the xinwei year of Longqing the master was begging in Fancun when he saw the quiet seclusion of Yunqi’s hills and waters, and formed the resolve to end his days there. The mountain was formerly the monastery of Chan Master Fuhu; Yang Guozhu, Chen Ruyu and others built him a thatched hut of three bays to dwell in. The master kept company with his own shadow on the cold cliff; once he went seven days without food, only sitting upright against the wall. The village had many tigers, and within forty li around the mountain no fewer than several dozen people were harmed each year; of all their troubles the people suffered most from this. The master roused his compassion and entreated on their behalf; he recited sutras and gave food offerings, and the tiger scourge was quieted. That year there was a severe drought, and the villagers begged the master to pray for rain. He laughed and said, “I know only Buddha-recitation; I have no other art.” The people insisted; unable to refuse, the master went out, and striking the wooden fish he walked along the fields reciting the Buddha’s name. Rain poured down at once, and fell wherever he went. The people marveled, and came in throngs, bringing timber and shouldering hoes and mattocks, vying to break ground there. They found a pillar base and pointed to it, saying, “This is a relic of the old Yunqi Monastery. The master has blessed our village; we wish to renew it, that our blessing may endure.” In a few days an aranya was finished — no lofty gate outside, no great hall within; only a Chan hall to settle the monks and a Dharma hall for the sutras and images, the rest serving merely to keep off wind and rain. From then on the Dharma flourished greatly; monks throughout the land turned their hearts to him, and it became a great monastery. The master grieved that in the age of the Dharma’s decline the net of the teachings was torn and broken, the way of Chan unclear, and beings’ karma deep and their defilements heavy — “to store sweet cream in a filthy vessel: this is what I fear.” Moreover, the Buddha established the three trainings to transform living beings, with the precepts as the foundation. If the foundation is not laid, on what do concentration and wisdom rest? Wishing to lead beings by what benefits them, one must first firm the root. But by the statutes of the realm the ordination platforms of north and south had long been banned and closed. Though he wished to revive the fallen discipline, how dared he defy the imperial decree? So he had the assembly recite the Brahma Net Precepts Sutra and the various precept chapters for monks every fortnight. Thereupon people near and far all turned to him. The master made strict observance of the vinaya his foremost practice, and wrote the Essential Outline for Novices, the Elementary Guide to the Full Precepts, and An Elucidation of the Brahma Net Sutra Commentary to make it clear. When the master first set out to travel and study, he gained power from investigating Buddha-recitation; by now he opened the single gate of the Pure Land, gathering all three capacities, and advocated it with all his strength. He therefore wrote the Commentary on the Amitābha Sūtra, more than a hundred thousand words, harmonizing the phenomenal and the principle and pointing all back to Mind Only. He also recalled having seen the recorded sayings of Gaofeng: in investigating this matter no one from of old had been keener or sharper — none surpassed this master, forged of pure steel. He had carried it with him on his wanderings. It was the master’s intent to unite this with Yongming of Mount Kuang in one. He further selected the most urgent sayings from the encounter dialogues of the ancient worthies and compiled them as Spurring On Through the Chan Gate, having it printed to show the secret of investigation. This reveals the dual cultivation of Chan and Pure Land, never departing from the one mind; thus one knows how subtle was the master’s skill in teaching. In the wuzi year of Wanli a great epidemic struck, and a thousand people died each day. The prefect Lord Yu Liangshu asked the master to go to Lingzhi Monastery to avert it, and the epidemic ceased. At Fancun there had once been the Zhu Bridge; the tides had washed it away, and travelers suffered in crossing. Lord Yu asked the master to take the lead in rebuilding it. The master said, “If you wish me to do it, then whether rich or poor, noble or base, each person shall give eight fen of silver and no more.” He used eight alone because the number eight is Kun, earth, and earth governs water. Someone said the work was great and the gifts small, and it would be hard to finish. The master said, “If the power of mind is great, the merit will of itself not decay.” In a few days a thousand gold pieces were amassed. Laborers were gathered and the foundation laid; for every pile driven he recited a mantra a hundred times, and for several days the tide did not come. The bridge was completed. Long ago King Qian shot at the tide with ten thousand crossbows; the master met it with the power of one mind. What art was this? The master’s standing in the Way grew daily, and monks from the ten directions came as if returning home. He received them all with kindness; disciples gathered daily and the dwelling grew cramped. The master did not care to adorn buildings but sought only what was peaceful and fit, mere shelters propped together. His pure regulations were set ever more strictly. The assembly had a common hall; those devoted to intensive practice, the aged and sick, and the visiting monks from the ten directions each had their own hall. Each of the hundred duties had its own quarters, every one with lock and key, opened and closed at set times. Each had its own words of warning, proclaimed at the appointed times. At night there were patrols; boards were struck and the Buddha’s name recited, the sound carrying through the valleys, so that even the weary could not sleep at ease and slept without dreams. At the fortnightly recitation and the karmic proceedings, merits and faults were brought up and rewards and penalties carried out, austere as ice and frost. Even when the Buddha dwelt at Jetavana there were still the six gangs troubling the assembly; here not one dared dispute or knowingly transgress. He did not bind himself wholly to Baizhang’s measuring line, yet he saved the ills of the age in good time; among monasteries ancient and modern there has never been one like this. All this is set out in the Monastic Regulations and the various admonitory texts, bright and clear. He was utterly intent on the precept against killing and honored the releasing of life; his writings on it long circulated in the world, and many within the seas honored and followed them. He once lectured on the Sutra of Perfect Enlightenment at Jingci Monastery; those who came to listen numbered thousands each day, ranged around him like a screen. He redeemed the Ten-Thousand-Labors Pond in front of the monastery as a life-releasing pond, and on the master’s eightieth birthday he enlarged it further. Today the two ponds, Shangfang and Changshou, in the city cost more than a hundred gold pieces a year. In the mountains a place for releasing life was established, where creatures that fly and walk were bought back and released until it was brimming; the monks cut down their own portions to feed them, at a yearly cost of about two hundred shi of grain. There were also words of warning for the keepers, proclaimed there at the appointed times. Even the feathered tribes, so fond of clamor, when they heard the sound of the wooden fish all fell silent and listened; when the recitation ended they beat their wings and sang aloud. Is this not Buddha-nature? Ah — the Buddha taught that filial piety itself is called precept, and the Confucians rebuked those who provide for parents without reverence. The master both provided for and revered living creatures, and did so with propriety: was this not filial piety brought to its fullest? The master’s way and style spread daily; the worthy and great within the seas, at court and in the countryside alike, all turned their hearts to him and were transformed. Such men as the Grand Marshal Lord Song Yingchang, the Grand Minister Lord Lu Guangzu, the Palace Admonisher Lord Zhang Yuanbian, the Director of Education Lord Feng Mengzhen, and Lord Tao Wangling came one after another to his gate to ask the Way, by the hundred; all knocked at the gate and beat time, inquiring into the great matter, and none failed to be won over, all entering his molding and forging. Provincial inspectors, prefects and ministers alighted from their carriages and bowed in salute, yet to the worthies and great men who came to pay their respects he gave no special courtesy and set out no feast. All were content with coarse rice and slept on worn mats, letting lizards crawl over them and mosquitoes bite without a change of countenance. All forgot their station and bent their power; those who came emptied themselves of what they had. Had his perfect sincerity not moved others, how could it have come to this? The Vice Minister Lord Wang Zongmu asked, “Last night a rat squeaked and squeaked and preached the whole Avatamsaka Sutra.” The master said, “What when the cat suddenly leaps out?” Wang had nothing to say, and the master answered for him: “The Dharma master runs off, and the lecture seat is left behind.” He also wrote a praise: “The rat squeaks and squeaks;
the Avatamsaka is clear and plain.
How strange is Vice Minister Wang —
deluded by a beast!
The cat springs out before the painted hall;
the bedside preaching leaves no word.
No word —
the Mahāvaipulya Buddhāvataṃsaka Sūtra, Chapter One, The Wondrous Adornments of the World Leaders.” The Censor Lord Zuo Zongying asked whether one could gain awakening by Buddha-recitation. The master said, “Turn the hearing to hear the self-nature; the nature accomplished, one attains the supreme Way. Why then doubt that turning the recitation back to recite the self-nature is the same?” The magistrate of Renhe, Lord Fan Liangshu, asked how a scattered mind could become still. The master said, “Set it in one place, and there is nothing that cannot be done.” A scholar in the assembly said, “To investigate one thing exclusively — that is setting it in one place. What can be accomplished by it?” The master said, “As for investigating things, one should simply follow Master Zhu’s sudden, thorough comprehension. What then cannot be accomplished?” Someone asked the master why he did not prize foreknowledge. The master said, “Suppose two men watch The Story of the Pipa. One has never seen it; the other has seen it and tells beforehand what will come. Yet they watch it through together — can he add or take away a single act?” The Emperor’s mother, the Empress Dowager Cisheng, revered the Three Jewels. Happening to see the master’s essay on releasing life, she greatly admired it, and sent palace attendants bearing a purple robe and funds for a vegetarian feast as offerings, and asked him for the essentials of the Dharma. The master bowed and accepted them, and answered with a verse. The master was utterly compassionate toward the suffering realms of the dark and unseen; he learned the flaming-mouth ritual himself and often performed it in person. Some once saw the Tathāgata’s form appear upon his seat — the power of his contemplation made it so. By nature the master was plain, simple and unadorned, without pretence; empty of heart, he responded to things. His countenance was gentle and pure, and he seemed too frail to bear the weight of his robes, yet his voice was like a great bell. His heart held no ramparts, yet he kept guard like a stern walled city and defended like steady troops. He was skilled at concealing his use; his patterns were fine and his scrutiny close; in ordering affairs great and small he let not a needle or a mustard seed slip by. Even in laying out the daily life of the monastery — measuring gifts and benefits, weighing what was fitting, checking causes and effects, making clear sin and merit, caring for the aged and sick, providing for the assembly of monks — not a drop of water was misdirected. In the fifty years since the monastery’s founding he never misspent a single coin. Though the resident community often numbered several thousand, he appointed no fundraiser, letting people come of themselves. Any surplus was at once distributed to other monasteries; the treasury held no savings. Whatever gold and silver came as offerings apart from the vegetarian feasts was scattered as soon as received, given for clothing and medicine, to relieve the poor and sick, with scarcely a day empty. Once he happened to examine his private accounts: in the last seven years he had in fact used more than five thousand gold pieces that did not belong to the permanent holdings — from which the years before may be known. Through life the master cherished his blessings, and once wrote thirty-two articles to warn himself. In old age he washed his own clothes and emptied his own chamber pot, not troubling an attendant. To the end of his life he wore plain cloth; a single hemp curtain, a thing from the days of his mother’s mourning, still survives. The rest may be known. Summing up the master’s conduct: with equal great compassion he gathered and transformed all. He spoke no word that was not the Buddha’s, did no deed that was not the Buddha’s, undertook no affair that was not the Buddha’s. The Buddha charged those who in the final age would protect the true Dharma to rely on the four practices of ease and bliss; the master truly lived by them. Looking through the patriarchs from of old: those who singly raised the true command did not necessarily cultivate all myriad practices. But as for one who manifests the one mind through the myriad practices, who sees the Buddha-nature within the dust of affliction — from ancient times to now, apart from Yongming, only the master alone. Earlier Confucians called Jiyin the Ban Gu and Sima Qian of monks; I would call the master the Zhou Gong and Confucius of the Dharma gate, for to bear the Dharma is to take up the Way. The master’s talent was enough to order the world, his awakening enough to transmit the mind, his teaching enough to fit the capacities at hand, his precepts enough to protect the Dharma, his conduct enough to rouse the age, his regulations enough to remedy its ills. As for kindness that gives joy and compassion that draws out suffering, and the wide practice of the six pāramitās — which of these is not a wondrous practice? From his leaving home to his end there was nothing to criticize; one may say that the Dharma gate gained one who embodied the Buddha’s whole substance and great function. Had he not been a great being of the manifestation body, lighting up the heavy darkness of the final age, how could he have come to this? At the end, half a month beforehand, he went into the city to take leave of his disciples and old friends, saying only, “I am going elsewhere.” Returning to the mountain, he came down into the hall again and again, set out tea and broth as offerings, and took leave of the assembly, saying, “I shall not stay here; I am going elsewhere.” At the mid-year festival the Ullambana basin was set out and each made offering for his ancestors. The master said, “This year I shall not join the gathering.” There was a register, and the master secretly wrote on it: “The duty monk of Yunqi Monastery makes offering on behalf of the late Master Lianchi of the hall, in memory of the Shen clan’s kin.” Only afterwards did they know that it was a sacrifice offered in advance. On the evening of the first of the seventh month he entered the hall and sat, charging the assembly: “You do not heed my words. I am like a candle in the wind; the lamp is out of oil. You will believe me only when I take one stumble and fall. Tomorrow I must go on a long journey.” The assembly urged him to stay. The master composed Three Regrets and Ten Lamentations to warn them. The layman Xu Lin of Songjiang and four others, five in all, were at the monastery, and he had an attendant deliver five copies of his testament. The following night he entered his quarters, showed a slight illness, and closed his eyes in silence. When his disciples from the city arrived and stood around him, he opened his eyes again and said, “All of you, recite the Buddha’s name honestly. Do not play the marvel-monger; do not ruin my regulations.” The assembly asked who should preside over the monastery. The master said, “One in whom precepts and practice are both complete.” They asked again about the present. The master said, “For now, follow the order of ordination.” Having spoken, he faced west, recited the Buddha’s name, and passed away upright. It was the noon hour of the fourth day of the seventh month in the forty-third year of Wanli. The master was born in the yiwei year of Jiajing; his worldly age was eighty-one, and his monastic years fifty. The master had himself chosen a spot below the ridge to the left of the monastery, and his whole body was enshrined there in a tower. His former wife, Madam Tang, afterwards shaved her head, founded the Xiaoyi Nunnery and became the head of a women’s monastery; she passed away one year before him, and her tower is also on the right-hand mountain outside the monastery. Among the master’s ordained disciples, Guangxiao and others were the foremost of the first generation. Those who came to his gate to receive precepts and ordination numbered no fewer than several thousand, not counting the laity. Officials and gentlemen who came to his gate likewise numbered in the thousands, not counting those who admired him from afar. His writings, apart from the sutra commentaries, include some twenty miscellaneous works such as the three volumes of Bamboo Window, all circulating in the world and all words of warning and awakening. The master always admonished his disciples to prize true practice and not to display marvels; therefore many of his spiritual wonders are not recorded here in full. Alas! I have heard that the World-Honored One, deeply mindful that living beings in the final age, though they be saved, might yet have their wisdom-life cut off, sought at the Vulture Peak assembly for those who would protect the true Dharma. Even those who had personally received the prediction dared not undertake it; only the host that sprang from the earth took it upon themselves. And they said, “We who uphold the sutra in the final age must possess great power of patience and great power of diligence. Even one who manifests a body here does not himself declare his origin or divulge the Buddha’s secret cause; only at the end, secretly, does he give a sign of it.” Looking at the master’s conduct — his hidden spirit and secret function, his power of patient endurance and diligence — is he not one of those who sprang from the earth? Or did he come from the Pure Land? Were it otherwise, then from the stage of an ordinary being, where even seeking one’s own benefit is not yet enough, how could one widely practice benefiting others and protect the true Dharma without defect from first to last? Moved by this, I have briefly gathered the master’s deeds to make them known to later ages; the rest is set out in the separate biographies. Thereupon I made this inscription, saying:

The essence of mountains and rivers makes culture shine;
The dharma's pulse flows hidden, clouds come, the valleys rush.
Here lies the spirit of She, long storing its wonder;
And so a wise one came and opened a single opening.
The dharma's teaching spread abroad, the mystic wind fanned each day;
In due season Master Zhu was born, his lofty standard bright as clouds.
A dragon of meaning in the ocean of teachings, a divine steed of the Chan school;
A single glance covers a thousand miles, lightning rolls, stars race.
He grasped the tally of going beyond, held the banner before words;
Hiding his radiance and veiling his light, he entered the boundless deep.
Embracing the Way and focusing spirit on green cliffs and emerald walls,
He sat and departed from them, his lone radiance blazing bright.
The stūpa's shadow props up the sky; the true wind brushes past;
The dharma-body stands majestic as clouds and mists come and go.

The master's monastic name was Zhiruo; his courtesy name was Yexi. He was a son of the Yao family of Shanyin; his mother was of the Yan family. She had first prayed to White-Robed Guanyin, and dreamed that a monk who was washing his feet told her, “I will make you a lion's cub.” She woke and was with child. He was keen and quick from birth, and as a child loved to sit cross-legged and recite the Buddha's name. His father died early and his mother lived in widowhood. He was only seven when his mother fell ill, and he wept bitterly day and night. When she was near death she charged him, saying, “You are a monk from a former life; do not betray your original vow.” With these words she passed away. Following his mother's dying command, the master sought out and bowed to Master Xian of Huayan Temple in Kuaiji and left home. At seventeen he was at last shaved and dyed. In his daily life he kept the great matter of birth and death close to heart, and he went straight to Niutou Mountain, resolving to investigate it. Before long he followed Master Jingshan and heard the Lotus Sutra expounded at Tiantai, and then hid himself in the mountains, straining with all his might at the matter of going beyond, dwelling alone for six years, until one day he touched a circumstance and had a breakthrough. At twenty-six he heard that Master Xuelang Hong'en had begun teaching in the southern capital, so with gourd and bamboo hat he set out. He first received full ordination from Master Su'an at Qixia, and then placed himself beneath Xuelang's seat, serving and studying for twelve years, exhaustively studying the scriptures and treatises and fathoming their profound mysteries. In the ji-yi year of Wanli, the Huihua Hermitage was founded at Zuili, and Sima Shen and Yue of the Ministry of Works invited the master to reside there. In the geng-yin year, when the master was thirty-six, Grand Minister Lu of Wutai, Assistant Censor Guan of Dongming, and Censor Liu Ziwei asked him to expound the Śūraṃgama Sutra at Wu Gate. In the ren-chen year he expounded the Lotus Sutra at Lingyin in Hangzhou, and the following year the Laṅkāvatāra Sutra at Jingci. In the ren-yin year he settled north of Feilai Peak in Wulin, where the old site of Yongfu Temple had fallen into private hands; Pan of the Court of Imperial Sacrifices redeemed and rebuilt it with a buddha-pavilion and a Chan hall, and so it became a hermitage. Throughout the three Wu and the two Zhe regions all took the master's teaching as their standard. At the monasteries where he stayed, he opened and expounded the scriptures and treatises in more than thirty places, and about fifty sessions in all, and he was called a master craftsman of his generation. The middle stretch of this record — the remainder of his later years, his death, and his disciples' request for this inscription — is corrupt in the source text. Of birth and death, firmly bound, there is none but is entangled;
How did the master see it? Thus, so free, so unconfined.
By seeds of prajñā, life after life perfumed and trained,
And so in coming and going he used no effort at all.
Moon of the precepts, flower of compassion,
Cloud of kindness, rain of dharma:
Benefiting self and benefiting others,
Pure and clean of any dust.
He fathomed the Buddha's mind, and spread his broad and long tongue;
Once the dharma's sound has reached the ear, its merit lasts for kalpas.
Alas, in this age of the dharma's decline, the master was a banner of the dharma;
May he long abide in the world, and demons and outsiders fall of themselves.
In birth and death, in coming and going, the dharma-body is still and quenched;
The master was truly unbound — this is true liberation.
The stūpa leans against a lone peak, and the sound of the pines never ceases;
Day and night, the perfect sound speaks on, blazing and ever constant.
